---
// Help: block identifiers (oscr/blocks.py, src/lib/block.ts, worker/block.ts, docs/PLAN_BLOCK_IDS.md). Every
// statement here is what the code does: how an identifier is made, where it shows, how it is cited and read.
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { API_BASE } from "../../lib/apispec";

const EXAMPLE = "oscr:b:ZJPAX6KPXPETB-7";
const toc = [
  { id: "what", text: "What a block identifier is" },
  { id: "made", text: "How it is made" },
  { id: "where", text: "Where you find it" },
  { id: "cite", text: "Citing a block" },
  { id: "page", text: "A block's page" },
  { id: "life", text: "Current, superseded, withdrawn" },
  { id: "api", text: "For programs" },
  { id: "limits", text: "What it is not" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Block identifiers"
  description={`The permanent identifier ${SITE_NAME} gives to each block of code linked to a paper: what it names, how it is made, and how to cite and read it.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/blocks/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    Each match between a paragraph of a paper and lines of its authors' code has a block identifier, such as{" "}
    <code>{EXAMPLE}</code>. It names those lines, in that file, linked to that paper, and it is never reused nor deleted:
    you can cite it, and its address keeps answering.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What a block identifier is</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>block</strong> is a run of lines of one file of the authors' code that the registry links to a paper: the
    lines that do what a paragraph of the paper describes. Its <strong>identifier</strong> is <code>oscr:b:</code>, then 13
    letters and digits, a dash and one check character. Its page is <a href={`/b/${EXAMPLE}`}><code>{`/b/${EXAMPLE}`}</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="made">How it is made</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It is computed from three facts: the paper's DOI, the file's <a href="https://www.swhid.org/">SWHID</a> (the Software
      Heritage identifier of the file as its authors published it, the same fingerprint as <code>git hash-object</code>),
      and the lines. The same block always gets the same identifier, and anyone can compute it again.
    </li>
    <li>
      The SWHID is computed from the exact bytes whose SHA-256 {SITE_NAME} verified, before any email address is hidden for
      display. A file whose text here is not exactly those bytes (cut short, or a notebook kept as its cells) gets no
      SWHID, and its matches get no block identifier: only what was verified is named.
    </li>
    <li>
      The 13 characters use Crockford's base32, without I, L, O or U, so it reads aloud and copies without confusion. The
      last character is a check (ISO 7064 MOD 37,36, the scheme ORCID uses): a mistyped identifier is reported as such,
      never sent to another block. Lower case, and an O written for a zero, are read correctly.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where">Where you find it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>On a paper's page, in the list of matches of the Code ↔ Paper reader: each match shows its block identifier.</li>
    <li>On a paper's page built on request, in the Code section, under "Code blocks with an identifier".</li>
    <li>In the citation the reader's Cite button copies, when the lines you selected are a block (see below).</li>
    <li>In the API and the bulk files: each match of <code>alignments.jsonl</code>, and each paper's record.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cite">Citing a block</h2>
  <p>
    In the reader, select a match (or lines inside it) and press <strong>Cite</strong>: the button reads "Cite block
    oscr:b:…", and it copies the block's identifier and page first, then the file and lines, the paper's DOI, the SWHID with
    its context and the link to these lines on the paper's page. For example:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`Code block ${EXAMPLE} (https://openscicode.org/b/${EXAMPLE})
fig_mtnn/mtnn.py, lines 316 to 451, in int-brain-lab/paper-reproducible-ephys (https://github.com/int-brain-lab/paper-reproducible-ephys), commit 2397f2c….
Paper: https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.100840
SWHID: swh:1:cnt:e5e096d4…;origin=…;anchor=swh:1:rev:…;path=/fig_mtnn/mtnn.py;lines=316-451
https://openscicode.org/paper/doi_10.7554_elife.100840/?path=fig_mtnn/mtnn.py#L316-L451`}</code></pre>
  <p>Lines that are no block are cited by their SWHID alone (<a href="/help/reader/#cite">citing a piece of code</a>).</p>

  <h2 id="page">A block's page</h2>
  <p>
    <code>/b/&lt;identifier&gt;</code> says what the block names: the paper and its page, the repository, the file, the
    lines and the function, the commit read, the SWHID, the paragraphs of the paper it was matched to, who linked it, and
    the day it was given. It links to these lines beside the paper. The same facts, for a program, are at{" "}
    <code>/b/&lt;identifier&gt;.json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="life">Current, superseded, withdrawn</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Current</strong>: the registry links these lines to the paper today.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Superseded</strong>: the code or the match changed, and these exact lines are no longer linked. The identifier
      stays and names what it named; its page links to the block of the same file that replaced it, if any.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Withdrawn</strong>: the paper left the registry, or a removal request withheld its code or its map. The page
      stays, and says so; it no longer shows the file, the lines or the code.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="api">For programs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GET /b/&lt;identifier&gt;.json</code> and <code>{`GET ${API_BASE}/blocks/<identifier>`}</code>: one block (CORS open).</li>
    <li><code>{`GET ${API_BASE}/paper/<doi>`}</code>: a paper's record, its blocks in <code>blocks</code>.</li>
    <li>The bulk <code>alignments.jsonl</code>: each match with its <code>block</code>. See <a href="/help/api/#e-block">the API guide</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">What it is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It is not a DOI. A tracing map validated by an author gets a DOI on Zenodo, and its file lists the identifiers of its
      blocks (<a href="/help/maps/">tracing maps</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      Most blocks are linked by the registry's matching program, which can be wrong: the page says who linked each one. You
      can report a wrong match from the reader ("Report a mismatch").
    </li>
    <li>
      The code stays its authors', under its own licence, at its source: the identifier names it and holds no copy.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Info>
